2
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:16,360
 I know why.

3
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:21,960
 Long duration isometrics don't look impressive, and they're f***ing brutal.

4
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:28,120
 You're not benching 405 or squatting 500 pounds, you're literally just not moving.

5
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:32,240
 But don't confuse nothing moving with nothing happening.

6
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:36,460
 Long duration isometrics can build serious strength and muscle.

7
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:40,800
 They're also one of my favorite ways to expose weaknesses and compensations.

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:42,220
 And here's the cool part.

9
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:48,280
 The exercise that exposes the weakness can also help fix the weakness.

10
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:51,140
 Isometrics can even have an analgesic effect.

11
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:55,680
 I've had athletes with patella tendonitis feel noticeably better after just two sets

12
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:00,360
 of iso hold lunges before training, and they're ridiculously versatile.

13
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:05,480
 You can program them in your warmup, your main workout, as a finisher, on a recovery

14
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,400
 day, even a stand alone workout.

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:16,040
 Some of my personal favorites are iso hold lunges, low push up holds, superman or Y holds,

16
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:20,600
 single leg back extension holds, and posterior chain planks.

17
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:22,680
 So why doesn't everybody do them?

18
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:25,440
 In my opinion, there's two main reasons.

19
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:28,920
 Number one is ego, and two is discomfort.

20
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:32,000
 Again, they don't look impressive.

21
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,360
 And when you push an iso hold into the two, three, or four plus minute range, they are

22
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:40,480
 absolutely f***ing miserable.

23
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:45,600
 But sometimes the exercises that look like you're doing nothing are doing a hell of a

24
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:46,600
 lot.

25
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,080
 So I highly recommend including these into your training.

